WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 96: The Gaspee Affair: The First Blood of the American Revolution

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.840
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.120
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:16.880
[Rebecca] Today, we sail into the choppy, fog-shrouded waters of Narragansett Bay, Rhode Island, to witness the forgotten first armed clash of the American Revolution.

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:23.1000
[Rebecca] The burning of the Royal Navy Customs schooner HMS Gaspy on June 9, 1772.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:40.240
[Rebecca] More than a year before Samuel Adams and the Sons of Liberty dumped British tea into Boston Harbor, Rhode Island merchants and sea captains took up muskets, rode under cover of midnight darkness, shot a Royal Navy lieutenant in the groin, and blew his patrol ship out of the water.

6
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:52.720
[Rebecca] When an enraged King George III threatened to drag the conspirators to England in chains for trial, the crisis united the 13 colonies through the committees of correspondence, lighting the fuse of revolution.

7
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.320
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full explosive story.

8
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:37.1000
[George] Its economy was built almost entirely upon international commerce, importing molasses from the French and Dutch West Indies, distilling it into rum in Newport and Providence distilleries, and trading rum and manufactured goods across the Atlantic.

9
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:48.320
[George] For generations, the British Crown had treated the colonies with salutary neglect, allowing Rhode Island merchants to evade imperial customs duties with impunity.

10
00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:58.320
[George] Bribery of royal customs collectors was common, and smuggling was viewed not as a criminal vice, but as a patriotic virtue that supported local employment.

11
00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:11.320
[George] However, following the conclusion of the costly Seven Years' War in 1763, a debt-burdened British Parliament resolved to enforce the Navigation Acts and Township duties with ruthless military efficiency.

12
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:22.680
[George] To police the defiant waters of Rhode Island, the British Admiralty dispatched the Royal Navy armed customs schooner HMS Gaspy, commanded by Lieutenant William Dudingston.

13
00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:31.000
[George] Armed with eight carriage guns and a crew of thirty British sailors, the Gaspy was tasked with hunting down illicit colonial cargo.

14
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.560
[George] But Lieutenant Dudingston was not merely an officer executing his orders.

15
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:43.480
[George] He was an arrogant, haughty martinet who despised American colonials and treated every merchant captain with intolerable contempt.

16
00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:52.840
[George] Merchants viewed British customs commissioners not as legitimate public servants, but as foreign extortionists preying on peaceful Rhode Island enterprise.

17
00:02:52.840 --> 00:03:02.680
[George] Arriving in Narragansett Bay in March 1772, Lieutenant William Dudingston quickly inaugurated a virtual reign of terror on the coastal waters.

18
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:14.440
[George] Operating outside recognized naval protocols, Dudingston stopped, boarded, and searched every colonial vessel he encountered, regardless of whether they carried legal manifests.

19
00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:28.520
[George] He seized small coastal market boats carrying firewood, vegetables and livestock from local farmers, confiscating their goods without explanation and insulting prominent town magistrates who dared to question his authority.

20
00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:40.840
[George] Adding fuel to the fire, Dudingston ignored Rhode Island law by refusing to present his royal commission to Governor Joseph Wanton in Newport, boasting that Rhode Islanders were a lawless pack of pirates.

21
00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:56.560
[George] When he seized the Rhode Island merchant sloop Fortune, loaded with twelve hogsheads of sugar and rum belonging to Nathanael Green, the future Revolutionary War General, Dudingston refused to have the forfeiture adjudicated in Rhode Island's Vice Admiralty Court.

22
00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:05.760
[George] Instead, he illegally shipped the vessel and its valuable cargo to Boston for condemnation, deeply offending Rhode Island's legal sovereignty.

23
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:14.000
[George] Governor Wanton sent sharp official letters demanding that Dudingston produce his commission and cease harassing peaceful colonial commerce.

24
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:24.400
[George] Dudingston responded with open mockery, while his superior, Admiral John Montagu in Boston, threatened to hang anyone who interfered with the Gaspy as a traitor.

25
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:38.880
[George] In Providence, prominent merchant John Brown and Sea Captain Abraham Whipple held furious private conferences, agreeing that if Lieutenant Dudingston ever gave them an opening, Rhode Island would teach the Royal Navy an unforgettable lesson.

26
00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:49.040
[George] Dudingston's abusive conduct alienated even staunch Tory loyalists, convincing the colony that Imperial Naval Power was operating outside the rule of law.

27
00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:55.040
[George] That fateful opening arrived on the morning of Tuesday, June 9, 1772.

28
00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:04.160
[George] Captain Benjamin Lindsay was commanding the packet's sloop Hanna, sailing on its regular commercial run from Newport up Narragansett Bay toward Providence.

29
00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:11.600
[George] Spotting the American merchant vessel, Lieutenant Dudingston signaled for the Hannah to heave to and submit to a boarding search.

30
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:22.800
[George] Captain Lindsay, familiar with every shallow reef and sandbar in the bay, deliberately refused to stop, hoisting his colours and letting out his sails to outrun the British schooner.

31
00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:31.080
[George] Infuriated by Lindsay's defiance, Dudingston ordered the Gaspy into hot pursuit, firing warning shots across the Hannah's bow.

32
00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:36.040
[George] For over twenty miles, the two vessels engaged in a high-speed chase up the bay.

33
00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:50.920
[George] Knowing that the tide was falling and that the Gaspy had a deeper draft than his own shallow sloop, Lindsay steered the Hannah directly across the treacherous underwater sand spit extending off Namquid Point, now known as Gaspy Point, near Warwick.

34
00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:56.440
[George] Lindsay's lightweight sloop skimmed safely across the shallow sandbar with inches to spare.

35
00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:05.960
[George] Minutes later, at shortly after three o'clock in the afternoon, the heavier Gaspy ploughed at full sail directly into the hidden sandbar with a bone-jarring crash.

36
00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:11.240
[George] The British schooner was hopelessly grounded, keeled over on its side in the wet mud.

37
00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:20.760
[George] With the tide receding rapidly, Dudingston realized his ship was immobilized and could not be refloated until the next high tide at three o'clock the following morning.

38
00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:29.000
[George] Captain Lindsay's tactical baiting of the British warship was hailed across Narragansett Bay as an audacious triumph of local seamanship.

39
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:40.280
[George] Reaching the docks of Providence at five o'clock in the afternoon, Captain Lindsay immediately rushed to the counting house of John Brown, the wealthiest and most influential merchant in town.

40
00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:52.120
[George] Lindsay reported the extraordinary news the hated Gaspy was helpless, stranded hard aground on Namquid Point six miles south of the city, perfectly vulnerable to a night assault.

41
00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:56.600
[George] John Brown recognized that this was the moment Providence had been waiting for.

42
00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:06.120
[George] He immediately dispatched a town crier through the streets with a drum, calling all interested citizens to assemble at James Sabin's tavern on South Main Street.

43
00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:14.200
[George] By dusk, the tavern was packed to overflowing with prominent sea captains, merchants, young apprentices, and sons of liberty.

44
00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:21.840
[George] In a back room around a roaring hearth, Brown and veteran privateer Captain Abraham Whipple organized the strike force.

45
00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:32.560
[George] Eight large, heavy wooden longboats, each equipped with five pairs of oars and capable of carrying a dozen men, were requisitioned from merchant wharves along the Providence River.

46
00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:46.240
[George] Men melted lead over the tavern fire to cast musket balls, sharpened boarding cutlasses, and wrapped the iron oarlocks of the boats in sheepskin and greasy rags to ensure complete silence as they rode through the midnight waters.

47
00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:56.000
[George] Inside Sabin's tavern, men from every station of colonial society pledged mutual secrecy, knowing that discovery meant death on an English scaffold.

48
00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:06.000
[George] At ten o'clock on the moonless night of June 9, eight longboats carrying over sixty heavily armed Rhode Island Patriots pushed silently away from Fenner's wharf.

49
00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:18.560
[George] Under the overall tactical command of Captain Abraham Whipple, the flotilla rowed down the ink-black waters of the Providence River in a single line, guided only by the rhythmic, muffled dipping of their oars.

50
00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:25.760
[George] Shortly after midnight, the dark, tilted silhouette of the stranded Gaspy loomed out of the fog near Namquid Point.

51
00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:33.280
[George] At sixty yards, a British sentinel on the deck spotted the approaching boats and challenged, Who comes there, Captain Whipple?

52
00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:36.880
[George] Standing in the bow of the lead boat, bellowed back.

53
00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:42.320
[George] I am the sheriff of the county of Kent, God damn you, and I have come to arrest you.

54
00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:49.760
[George] Lieutenant Dudingston rushed onto the deck in his nightshirt, brandishing a cutlass and shouting for the crew to man the swivel guns.

55
00:08:49.760 --> 00:09:02.360
[George] But before the British sailors could fire, a young road islander in the bow named Joseph Bucklin raised his musket, turned to his companion, and said, F, reach me your gun, and I will kill that man.

56
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:03.560
[George] Bucklin fired.

57
00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:11.640
[George] The lead ball tore through Dudingston's groin and abdomen, dropping the British lieutenant instantly to the deck in a pool of blood.

58
00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:21.720
[George] With a ferocious battle cry, the Rhode Islanders rammed their longboats against the Gaspies wooden hull, swarming over the gunnels with cutlasses and boarding pikes.

59
00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:31.880
[George] Surprised, outnumbered, and terrified by their commander's fall, the British crew surrendered after a brief, violent struggle without firing a single cannon.

60
00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:40.920
[George] The swiftness and silence of the longboat assault completely paralyzed the Royal Navy crew, preventing them from discharging a single swivel gun.

61
00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:47.320
[George] Once in total command of the captured schooner, the Rhode Islanders demonstrated remarkable discipline.

62
00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:49.240
[George] Captain Whipple ordered young Dr.

63
00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:55.800
[George] John Mawney, who had joined the expedition with his medical instruments, to tend to the wounded British lieutenant.

64
00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:03.720
[George] In the ship's cabin, Mawney dressed Dudingston's severe groin wound and staunched the bleeding, saving the royal officer's life.

65
00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:11.640
[George] Whipple ordered the captured British crew and their wounded lieutenant loaded into the longboats along with their personal clothing and sea chests.

66
00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:19.000
[George] They were rowed safely ashore and landed at Potuxet village, where Dudingston was placed in the care of a local family.

67
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:30.040
[George] Meanwhile, back on the grounded schooner, Brown and Whipple's men systematically searched the vessel for Royal Customs Manifests and official documents, confiscating all papers.

68
00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:35.800
[George] They then smashed open casks of rum and pitch, soaking the cabin, decks, and rigging.

69
00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:38.200
[George] Fire was set to the forward hold.

70
00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:48.320
[George] As the eight longboats rowed back into the dark bay toward Providence, flames engulfed the wooden schooner from bow to stern, lighting the midnight sky for miles around.

71
00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:52.000
[George] At dawn, the fire reached the Gaspies powder magazine.

72
00:10:52.240 --> 00:11:01.200
[George] A colossal explosion tore the ship apart, sending shattered timber and iron cannons into the sky and burning the despised vessel down to the waterline.

73
00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:09.520
[George] The fiery destruction of the Gaspi illuminated the shores of Warwick and Providence, signaling the end of passive colonial submission.

74
00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:17.520
[George] When news of the violent destruction of a Royal Navy warship reached London, King George III and his ministers were incensed.

75
00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:31.920
[George] The Crown issued an extraordinary royal proclamation offering a massive reward of 1,000 pounds for the apprehension of the ringleaders and created a special royal commission of inquiry composed of Imperial Admiralty judges and colonial governors.

76
00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:50.640
[George] Most alarmingly, the Crown decreed that any person indicted in the burning of the Gaspy would be stripped of their constitutional right to a local jury trial, placed in irons, and transported across the Atlantic to England to face trial for high treason, a crime punishable by drawing and quartering.

77
00:11:50.640 --> 00:12:00.480
[George] Yet, despite the fact that hundreds of Providence residents knew precisely who had planned and executed the attack, not a single Rhode Islander ever broke silence.

78
00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:10.000
[George] For six months, the Royal Commission interrogated local merchants and fishermen, but encountered a wall of absolute, impenetrable collective amnesia.

79
00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:12.720
[George] Not a single person was ever prosecuted.

80
00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:20.720
[George] The threat to transport American colonists to England for treason galvanized patriotic outrage across all thirteen colonies.

81
00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:37.960
[George] In Virginia, young Burgess's Thomas Jefferson, Patrick Henry, and Richard Henry Lee were so alarmed by the Royal Commission that they established the permanent intercolonial committees of correspondence, creating the vital communications network that coordinated the continental resistance.

82
00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:43.640
[George] The burning of the Gaspi on June 10, 1772 was not a mere riot.

83
00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:50.600
[George] It was the crossing of the Rubicon, the first organized armed attack against the armed forces of the British Empire.

84
00:12:50.600 --> 00:13:02.520
[George] Today, Rhode Island proudly celebrates Gaspi Day every June with parades and commemorative bonfires, honoring the bold sea dogs who dared to shed the first blood of American liberty.

85
00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:12.120
[George] Rhode Island's armed defiance inspired patriots throughout the colonies, proving that ordinary citizens could strike back against imperial tyranny.

86
00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:37.400
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we enter the glittering summer palaces of Gilded Age New York.

